    <md:title>9.9.4: Rewriting Quadratics</md:title>
    <md:uuid>58d8701b-dc61-440b-b912-5d1279518624</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>For questions 1 - 2, use the function \(f(x)=x^2\) that is shifted to create the function \(g(x)\) that has a vertex at \((3, -2)\).</para>

<!--Q#1-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Write \(g(x)\) in vertex form. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(g(x)=(x-3)^2-2\)
      The vertex form of a quadratic is \(f(x)=a(x-h)^2+k\).
      Since there is not a vertical stretch or compression or reflection mentioned, the vertex values are substituted for \((h, k)\).
      \(g(x)=(x-3)^2+-2\) becomes \(g(x)=(x-3)^2-2\).</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#2-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Write \(g(x)\) in standard form. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Compare your answer: \(g(x)=x^2-6x+7\)
      \(g(x)=(x-3)^2-2\)
      \(g(x)=(x-3)(x-3)-2\)
      \(g(x)=x^2-3x-3x+9-2\)
      \(g(x)=x^2-6x+7\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
</section>
